MyGo patch 0003 (Auralink): WindowOptions.NonActivating.

A floating window over the app the user dictates into (Auralink's
recording pill, its answer card) must take clicks on its buttons without
activating its app: activation moves the keyboard focus (and the caret)
away from the target app, and ⌘V would then paste into the wrong window.
AppKit honours NSWindowStyleMaskNonactivatingPanel only on an NSPanel,
and the window server learns it only at init; swapping the class of a
live window (object_setClass, as tauri-nspanel does) proved unreliable:
after the first click, clicks stopped reaching the app at all.

MyGo 0.5.0's WindowOptions.Panel creates such a panel (MyGoPanel) for
launchers, but one that takes the keyboard when shown, focused or clicked
(canBecomeKeyWindow), as Spotlight's: a click on it would take the keys
from the app the user dictates into. NonActivating is the panel that never
does.

The platform options already carry Focusable (false gives Windows
WS_EX_NOACTIVATE; flyouts use it), so the public option maps onto it:
the macOS backend creates a MyGoNonactivatingPanel (never key, never main,
floating, not hidden when its app deactivates) with the style bit at init,
and tracks the mouse over it always, not only while its app is active, so
hover works while another app is in front. With Panel as well, it is this
panel. Linux ignores the option.

Measured on macOS 26.7 (Auralink's M0 spike S3): a click on such a panel
reaches the button 7 times out of 7 while NSApp stays inactive and the
target app keeps its key window and caret.

diff --git a/internal/darwin/surface.go b/internal/darwin/surface.go
index ddfca65..6e960fd 100644
--- a/internal/darwin/surface.go
+++ b/internal/darwin/surface.go
@@ -156,7 +156,12 @@ func (w *window) createSurface(content NSRect) {
 	layer := send(s.view, "layer")
 	send(layer, "setContentsGravity:", uintptr(nsString("resize")))
 	// Tracking for hover, inside the visible part of the view.
-	const options = 0x01 | 0x02 | 0x04 | 0x40 | 0x200 // entered/exited, moved, cursor update, active app, visible rect
+	options := uint(0x01 | 0x02 | 0x04 | 0x40 | 0x200) // entered/exited, moved, cursor update, active app, visible rect
+	if w.opts.Flyout == nil && !w.opts.Focusable {
+		// A non-activating window (WindowOptions.NonActivating) works
+		// while another app is active: hover it all the same.
+		options = options&^0x40 | 0x80 // NSTrackingActiveAlways
+	}
 	s.tracking = msgInitTracking(send(class("NSTrackingArea"), "alloc"), sel("initWithRect:options:owner:userInfo:"), NSRect{}, options, s.view, 0)
 	send(s.view, "addTrackingArea:", uintptr(s.tracking))
 	if respondsTo(s.view, "displayLinkWithTarget:selector:") {
diff --git a/internal/darwin/window.go b/internal/darwin/window.go
index e77de0a..3639bd7 100644
--- a/internal/darwin/window.go
+++ b/internal/darwin/window.go
@@ -135,7 +135,13 @@ func (w *window) create() {
 		content.Origin = NSPoint{}
 	}
 	cls := "MyGoWindow"
-	if o.Panel {
+	switch {
+	case !o.Focusable:
+		// WindowOptions.NonActivating: the style must be there at init,
+		// for the window server to know the window never activates.
+		cls = "MyGoNonactivatingPanel"
+		style |= styleNonactivatingPanel
+	case o.Panel:
 		// The nonactivating style must be there from the start: the window
 		// server only learns that the window never activates its app from
 		// the style it is created with.
@@ -145,6 +151,11 @@ func (w *window) create() {
 	w.win = msgInitWindow(send(class(cls), "alloc"), sel("initWithContentRect:styleMask:backing:defer:"),
 		content, style, nsBackingStoreBuffered, false)
 	send(w.win, "setReleasedWhenClosed:", 0)
+	if !o.Focusable {
+		send(w.win, "setFloatingPanel:", 1)
+		send(w.win, "setBecomesKeyOnlyIfNeeded:", 1)
+		send(w.win, "setHidesOnDeactivate:", 0)
+	}
 	w.delegate = alloc("MyGoWindowDelegate")
 	send(w.win, "setDelegate:", uintptr(w.delegate))
 	send(w.win, "setTitle:", uintptr(nsString(o.Title)))
@@ -1399,6 +1410,14 @@ func registerWindowClasses() {
 		method("selectKeyViewPrecedingView:", selectKeyView("MyGoPanel", true)),
 	})
 
+	// A non-activating window (WindowOptions.NonActivating) is a panel
+	// that never becomes the key or the main window: clicks reach its
+	// content and leave the app in front, and its first responder alone.
+	classDef("MyGoNonactivatingPanel", "NSPanel", nil, []objc.MethodDef{
+		method("canBecomeKeyWindow", func(self id, _ objc.SEL) bool { return false }),
+		method("canBecomeMainWindow", func(self id, _ objc.SEL) bool { return false }),
+	})
+
 	// A flyout's panel becomes the key window only when it takes the
 	// keyboard, and never the main window: its parent stays it.
 	classDef("MyGoFlyout", "NSPanel", nil, []objc.MethodDef{
diff --git a/window.go b/window.go
index 137fbd9..fcd1784 100644
--- a/window.go
+++ b/window.go
@@ -117,6 +117,15 @@ type WindowOptions struct {
 	Maximized bool
 	// SkipTaskbar hides the window from the taskbar (Linux, Windows).
 	SkipTaskbar bool
+	// NonActivating makes a window that never activates the app nor takes
+	// the keyboard: a press on it reaches its content while the app in
+	// front keeps the focus and its caret, as for a floating HUD over the
+	// text the user is typing into (dictation, a color picker). On macOS
+	// the window is an NSPanel with NSWindowStyleMaskNonactivatingPanel
+	// that never becomes the key or main window; on Windows it has
+	// WS_EX_NOACTIVATE. Linux ignores it. Its content cannot take the
+	// keyboard: no text inputs.
+	NonActivating bool
 	// AutoHideMenuBar keeps the window's menu bar out of sight until the
 	// user presses Alt or F10, and hides it again once they leave it
 	// (Linux, Windows). Its keyboard shortcuts work all along.
@@ -496,7 +505,7 @@ func (w *Window) platformOptions(o *WindowOptions) *platform.WindowOptions {
 		Minimizable:    !o.DisableMinimize,
 		Maximizable:    !o.DisableMaximize,
 		Closable:       !o.DisableClose,
-		Focusable:      true,
+		Focusable:      !o.NonActivating,
 		Fullscreenable: !o.DisableFullScreen,
 		AlwaysOnTop:    o.AlwaysOnTop,
 		FullScreen:     o.FullScreen,
